       Standard C library (_l_i_b_c, _-_l_c)

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ##iinncclluuddee <<ffccnnttll..hh>>          /* Definition of AATT__** constants */
       ##iinncclluuddee <<ssyyss//mmoouunntt..hh>>

       iinntt ffssppiicckk((iinntt _d_i_r_f_d,, ccoonnsstt cchhaarr **_p_a_t_h,, uunnssiiggnneedd iinntt _f_l_a_g_s));;

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  ffssppiicckk() system call is part of the suite of file‐descriptor‐based
       mount facilities in Linux.

       ffssppiicckk() creates a new filesystem configuration context for the  extant
       filesystem  instance  associated  with  the path described by _d_i_r_f_d and
       _p_a_t_h, places it into reconfiguration mode (similar to mmoouunntt(8) with the
       --oo rreemmoouunntt option).  A new file descriptor associated with the filesys‐
       tem configuration context is then returned.  The calling  process  must
       have  the  CCAAPP__SSYYSS__AADDMMIINN capability in order to create a new filesystem
       configuration context.

       The resultant file descriptor can be used with ffssccoonnffiigg(2)  to  specify
       the  desired  set of changes to filesystem parameters of the filesystem
       instance.  Once the desired set of changes have  been  configured,  the
       changes   can   be   effectuated   by   calling  ffssccoonnffiigg(2)  with  the
       FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__RREECCOONNFFIIGGUURREE command.  In  contrast  to  the  behaviour  of
       MMSS__RREEMMOOUUNNTT with mmoouunntt(2), ffssppiicckk() instantiates the filesystem configu‐
       ration  context with a copy of the extant filesystem’s filesystem para‐
       meters; thus, subsequent FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__RREECCOONNFFIIGGUURREE operations will  only
       update filesystem parameters explicitly modified with ffssccoonnffiigg(2).

       As  with "*at()" system calls, ffssppiicckk() uses the _d_i_r_f_d argument in con‐
       junction with the _p_a_t_h argument to determine the path to operate on, as
       follows:

       •  If the pathname given in _p_a_t_h is absolute, then _d_i_r_f_d is ignored.

       •  If the pathname given in _p_a_t_h is relative and _d_i_r_f_d is  the  special
          value  AATT__FFDDCCWWDD,  then  _p_a_t_h  is interpreted relative to the current
          working directory of the calling process (like ooppeenn(2)).

       •  If the pathname given in _p_a_t_h is relative, then  it  is  interpreted
          relative  to  the directory referred to by the file descriptor _d_i_r_f_d
          (rather than relative to the current working directory of the  call‐
          ing  process,  as  is  done by ooppeenn(2) for a relative pathname).  In
          this case, _d_i_r_f_d must be a directory that  was  opened  for  reading
          (OO__RRDDOONNLLYY) or using the OO__PPAATTHH flag.

       •  If  _p_a_t_h  is  an empty string, and _f_l_a_g_s contains FFSSPPIICCKK__EEMMPPTTYY__PPAATTHH,
          then the file descriptor _d_i_r_f_d is operated  on  directly.   In  this
          case, _d_i_r_f_d may refer to any type of file, not just a directory.

       See ooppeennaatt(2) for an explanation of why the _d_i_r_f_d argument is useful.

       _f_l_a_g_s  can be used to control aspects of how _p_a_t_h is resolved and prop‐
       erties of the returned file descriptor.  A  value  for  _f_l_a_g_s  is  con‐
       structed by bitwise ORing zero or more of the following constants:

              FFSSPPIICCKK__CCLLOOEEXXEECC
                     Set  the  close‐on‐exec (FFDD__CCLLOOEEXXEECC) flag on the new file
                     descriptor.  See the description of the OO__CCLLOOEEXXEECC flag in
                     ooppeenn(2) for reasons why this may be useful.

              FFSSPPIICCKK__EEMMPPTTYY__PPAATTHH
                     If _p_a_t_h is an empty string, operate on the file  referred
                     to  by  _d_i_r_f_d (which may have been obtained from ooppeenn(2),
                     ffssmmoouunntt(2), or ooppeenn__ttrreeee(2)).  In this  case,  _d_i_r_f_d  may
                     refer  to  any  type  of  file, not just a directory.  If
                     _d_i_r_f_d is AATT__FFDDCCWWDD, ffssppiicckk() will operate on  the  current
                     working directory of the calling process.

              FFSSPPIICCKK__SSYYMMLLIINNKK__NNOOFFOOLLLLOOWW
                     Do not follow symbolic links in the terminal component of
                     _p_a_t_h.   If  _p_a_t_h references a symbolic link, the returned
                     filesystem context will reference the filesystem that the
                     symbolic link itself resides on.

              FFSSPPIICCKK__NNOO__AAUUTTOOMMOOUUNNTT
                     Do not automount the terminal ("basename")  component  of
                     _p_a_t_h  if  it  is  a directory that is an automount point.
                     This allows you to reconfigure an automount point, rather
                     than the location that would be mounted.  This  flag  has
                     no effect if the automount point has already been mounted
                     over.

       As with filesystem contexts created with ffssooppeenn(2), the file descriptor
       returned  by ffssppiicckk() may be queried for message strings at any time by
       calling rreeaadd(2) on the file descriptor.  (See  the  "Message  retrieval
       interface" subsection in ffssooppeenn(2) for more details on the message for‐
       mat.)

